"During a shower, trace the meteors backward, and they all seem to come from one spot in the sky, called the radiant. The grains are really moving side by side along parallel paths, but perspective makes them look as if they spread out from a single point, just as railroad tracks seem to meet in the distance. Each shower is named after the constellation where its radiant lies: the Perseids come from Perseus, and the Leonids from Leo. More meteors fall after midnight, when your side of Earth faces forward along its orbit, like the windshield of a car driving through rain.": "508ef45579e6",
"Earth's orbit crosses some of these trails. Every year, when Earth reaches the same point in its orbit, it plows through the same trail of dust, and we see a meteor shower. That is why each shower returns on about the same dates every year: the Perseids in August, the Orionids in October, and the Geminids in December. Halley's Comet gives us two showers, because Earth passes close to its orbit twice a year, in May and in October.": "7b84f2d032c3",
"On a dark night, you may see a quick streak of light shoot across the sky. People call it a shooting star, but it is not a star at all. It is a meteor: a speck of space dust, often smaller than a grain of sand, slamming into Earth's air at up to 70 kilometers per second. The air in front of it gets so hot that it glows, about 100 kilometers above the ground, and the tiny grain burns up in less than a second.": "177b23d0460c",
"On a dark night, you may see a quick streak of light shoot across the sky. People call it a shooting star, but it is not a star at all. It is a meteor: a speck of space dust, often smaller than a grain of sand, slamming into Earth's air at up to 70 kilometers per second. The air in front of it gets so hot that it glows, about 100 kilometers above the ground, and the tiny grain burns up in less than a second. Where does all that dust come from? Most of it comes from comets. A comet is a chunk of ice and rock a few kilometers across that travels around the Sun on a long, stretched orbit. When it comes close to the Sun, some of the ice turns to gas and blows dust out into space. Over hundreds and thousands of years, the dust spreads out along the comet's orbit like a trail of crumbs. Earth's orbit crosses some of these trails. Every year, when Earth reaches the same point in its orbit, it plows through the same trail of dust, and we see a meteor shower. That is why each shower returns on about the same dates every year: the Perseids in August, the Orionids in October, and the Geminids in December. Halley's Comet gives us two showers, because Earth passes close to its orbit twice a year, in May and in October. During a shower, trace the meteors backward, and they all seem to come from one spot in the sky, called the radiant. The grains are really moving side by side along parallel paths, but perspective makes them look as if they spread out from a single point, just as railroad tracks seem to meet in the distance. Each shower is named after the constellation where its radiant lies: the Perseids come from Perseus, and the Leonids from Leo. More meteors fall after midnight, when your side of Earth faces forward along its orbit, like the windshield of a car driving through rain. People have watched meteor showers for thousands of years. In 687 BCE, the Chinese chronicle known as the Spring and Autumn Annals recorded that in the middle of the night, \"stars fell like rain,\" one of the oldest records of a meteor shower anywhere. In November 1833, the Leonids filled the sky over North America with tens of thousands of meteors an hour. The American scientist Denison Olmsted noticed that they all came from one point in Leo and concluded that they must come from space. In 1866, the Italian astronomer Giovanni Schiaparelli showed that the Perseids follow the same path as a comet, Swift–Tuttle. You do not need a telescope to watch a meteor shower. Your eyes are the best tool, because they can take in the whole sky at once. Find a dark place away from city lights, lie back, and give your eyes about 20 minutes to get used to the dark. Be patient: meteors often come in bursts, with quiet minutes in between. A bright Moon washes out the fainter meteors, so check the list below to see when the next showers peak over Changhua and how bright the Moon will be.": "29c0609bc7c7",
"People have watched meteor showers for thousands of years. In 687 BCE, the Chinese chronicle known as the Spring and Autumn Annals recorded that in the middle of the night, \"stars fell like rain,\" one of the oldest records of a meteor shower anywhere. In November 1833, the Leonids filled the sky over North America with tens of thousands of meteors an hour. The American scientist Denison Olmsted noticed that they all came from one point in Leo and concluded that they must come from space. In 1866, the Italian astronomer Giovanni Schiaparelli showed that the Perseids follow the same path as a comet, Swift–Tuttle.": "5f98f9448e2f",
